  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-06-surd-meaning-order-laws",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Surd Meaning, Order, and Basic Laws",
    "description": "Recognize surds, identify their order, and simplify roots using the chapter’s product, quotient and power laws.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start by separating perfect roots from genuine irrational surds. Then make each surd law concrete with small roots before extracting perfect square or cube factors and combining like surds.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach the chapter definition of a surd and its order, contrasting roots that stay irrational with perfect roots that become rational. Connect nth roots to fractional indices, then use the stated laws n√(ab)=n√a·n√b, n√(a/b)=n√a/n√b, and (n√a)^n=a. Build from square-root products and quotients to extracting perfect square or cube factors, then combining like surds after simplification. Learners should first ask whether the root is already perfect, then look for the largest perfect-power factor inside the radical."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A root sign does not automatically mean 'surd'.\n\n√49 is simply 7, so it is rational. √50 stays irrational, so it is a surd. The first skill is knowing what kind of root you are looking at before simplifying it.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The order of a surd is the root number.\n\n∛5 is a surd of order 3 because its cube root is irrational.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Likewise, ⁴√7 has order 4.\n\nThe order is not the value inside the root; it tells which power would undo the radical.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
